<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        A scene is a sequence of lighting steps, such as a chase or a winner's celebration. Scenes belong to a
        kiosk and are listed on the desk.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Making a scene">
        <ol>
            <li>On the desk, press <strong>New scene</strong> and give it a name. Its editor opens. (The pencil next to
                a scene opens the editor later.)</li>
            <li>Press <strong>Add step</strong>. A step is one look: the levels the lights go to, and how long they hold.</li>
            <li>Set the step with the fixture cards or the <strong>Channels</strong> faders below. Everything saves as
                you go; the top bar says <strong>Saved</strong>.</li>
            <li>Set how long the step lasts in <strong>Seconds</strong> (fractions work, such as 0.25 for a fast chase), and
                give it a <strong>Name</strong> if you like.</li>
            <li>To blend into a step instead of snapping to it, set <strong>Fade in (s)</strong>. The fade is part of the
                step's length, not extra: a 2 second step with a 1 second fade fades for 1 second, then holds for 1. A fade
                longer than the step is cut to fit.</li>
            <li>Add more steps. Each step shows small coloured blocks for how the lights will look.</li>
        </ol>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Working on steps">
        <ul>
            <li>Drag a step to move it, or use the arrows. <strong>Duplicate</strong> copies a step to just after itself,
                and <strong>Delete step</strong> removes it.</li>
            <li><strong>Capture lights</strong> makes the step look exactly like the lights do right now, so you can set
                a look on the desk first and then save it into a step.</li>
            <li>On the <strong>Channels</strong> tab, dimmed faders aren't part of the step: when it plays, those channels
                are left as they are (which is different from 0). Move a fader to add it; press <strong>×</strong> to
                take a channel out.</li>
            <li>The editor starts <strong>Blind</strong>, so the lights don't change while you work. Choose
                <strong>Show on lights</strong> to see the selected step on the kiosk.</li>
            <li><strong>Preview</strong> plays the scene on screen only. <strong>Run on kiosk</strong> plays it for real.
                Changing a running scene stops it, so it never plays an old version.</li>
            <li><strong>Delete</strong> at the top removes the whole scene, and tells you first if an automation rule runs it.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Scene settings">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Loop</strong>: start again from the first step after the last.</li>
            <li><strong>Momentary</strong>: the scene only runs while you hold its play button down, like a flash button.</li>
            <li><strong>BPM sync</strong>: change steps on the beat of the song that's playing. Each step then lasts its
                <strong>Beats</strong>. When nothing is playing, steps use their fixed duration.</li>
            <li><strong>Keybinds</strong>: keyboard keys that run the scene while the desk is open. If another scene
                already uses the key you press, the editor says so (pressing it then runs both). Tab and Enter can't be used.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Running scenes">
        <FluentText>
            Press <strong>Run</strong> (or <strong>Stop</strong>) next to a scene. A momentary scene has a
            <strong>Hold</strong> button instead: it runs while you hold it. A bar shows how far through a running
            scene is, and if a scene stops because of a problem the reason shows under its name.
            <strong>Stop all</strong> stops every scene on the kiosk. Scenes also run from an automation rule's
            <strong>DMX Scene Control</strong> action (for example a team's colours when it wins), or with their
            keybind, shown on the scene as a key.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
